        "text": "{\"scores\":{\"center\":5,\"craft\":4,\"facts\":5,\"age_fit\":5,\"continuity\":5,\"delight\":4},\"evidence\":{\"center\":\"Lennon acts in every level. He presses Enter, whacks the fading dot back over the net, flies the Spacewar ship, holds Ralph's overlay, stands on a stool to organise the arcade kids and asks \\\"What saved them?\\\" in the desert. His likes drive the climax: he opens Roblox Studio mid-air and builds \\\"a goal. Then a penalty spot,\\\" which uses both Roblox and soccer.\",\"craft\":\"There is a strong cold open at the late-night computer and a clear device (\\\"PRESS ENTER TO START\\\"). The coin-collecting structure pays off as a trophy, and the ending calls back with \\\"THE NEXT LEVEL IS YOURS TO BUILD.\\\" But the deletion threat vanishes for Levels 6-8, which become sightseeing stops. Lennon's big realisation that levels were saved because \\\"somebody made something new\\\" also sits awkwardly with the Pong and arcade levels, which were saved by playing.\",\"facts\":\"The facts are accurate throughout: Higinbotham 1958, Spacewar! and its sharing, the Odyssey overlays, Andy Capp's jammed coin box, Nishikado, Iwatani, Jumpman, the 2014 E.T. dig, Pajitnov and the Game Boy, Interactive Physics, and DynaBlocks to Roblox in 2006. Legends are hedged (\\\"That might be a bit of an exaggeration\\\", \\\"He said he got the idea from a pizza\\\"). The True Facts list matches the story.\",\"age_fit\":\"At 2471 words the story is within range. The tone suits a 10-year-old, with dry asides (\\\"Honestly? Respect.\\\", \\\"Multiplayer,\\\" corrected Lennon. \\\"Obviously.\\\"). A big word, \\\"oscilloscope\\\", is explained in-story. The peril is mild and each level resolves it.\",\"continuity\":\"This is the first story with no bible. Bit, the coins and the level structure stay internally consistent, including the count of seven coins.\",\"delight\":\"There are lots of lines a parent will enjoy reading: \\\"a dial-up modem having a sneeze\\\", \\\"The graphics were a sticker,\\\" \\\"It's not broken, it's full,\\\" and the Tetris under-the-covers wink. But the last line is duplicated after \\\"The End\\\", which reads like a glitch. Bit's \\\"8-bit, which is a joke you'll understand later\\\" is also never paid off.\"},\"caps_applied\":[\"none\"],\"disqualified\":false,\"overall\":4.65,\"editor_notes\":[\"Keep the deletion threat alive in Levels 6-8. Give Lennon a hands-on save in each, for example helping the NES kid clear a glitching Mario level or slotting a Tetris piece to stop the fade, so the middle doesn't turn into a museum tour.\",\"Align Lennon's climactic insight with what actually happened. Either have earlier saves involve making or fixing something, or change the line so it honours both playing and building.\",\"Remove the repeated \\\"Tomorrow, he had a game to make.\\\" after \\\"The End\\\", and either pay off Bit's 8-bit joke or cut it.\"],\"best_moment\":\"Allan stared. \\\"It's not broken,\\\" he said slowly. \\\"It's *full*.\\\"\",\"worst_moment\":\"## The End\\n\\nTomorrow, he had a game to make.\"}"
